course: biology
lesson: 126
title: The Lion King's hyenas, Rafiki and Zazu: real hyenas, mandrills and hornbills
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In The Lion King the hyenas are cackling, lazy thieves, Rafiki is a wise old mandrill on the savanna, and Zazu is a hornbill who flies about reporting news.
explain: Real spotted hyenas are skilled hunters that run down zebras and wildebeest, and in some places lions steal from hyenas more often than hyenas steal from lions.
explain: A hyena clan is ruled by females, who are bigger than the males, and the famous «laugh» is a giggle of nerves or excitement, not of fun.
explain: Real mandrills live in the rainforests of Central Africa, not on the savanna, and have short stumpy tails; Rafiki's long tail and home are more like a baboon's.
explain: Real red-billed hornbills hop about the savanna for insects and seeds, team up with dwarf mongooses, and the mother seals herself into a tree hole with mud to keep her chicks safe.
fact: Spotted hyenas are skilled hunters that catch most of their own food in many places, and in the Ngorongoro Crater lions ate hyena kills more often than the other way round.
fact: A spotted hyena's giggle usually means it is nervous, excited or frustrated, not happy; its «whoop» can be heard several kilometres away.
fact: In spotted hyena clans the females are in charge and are bigger and stronger than the males; a clan can have up to about 80 members.
fact: Spotted hyenas have some of the strongest bites of any mammal and can crush and digest bones, so their dry droppings are white with calcium.
fact: Hyenas look like dogs but are more closely related to cats and mongooses; the aardwolf is a small hyena that eats mostly termites.
fact: Mandrills live in the rainforests of Central Africa and have short stumpy tails; they are the biggest monkeys in the world.
fact: Male mandrills have the brightest red and blue faces, and the most important male in a group has the strongest colours.
fact: Mandrills have very long canine teeth, and a wide yawn shows them off as a warning to rivals.
fact: Red-billed hornbills live in dry African savannas, eating insects, seeds and small animals on the ground; the big bill is light because it is hollow inside.
fact: A female red-billed hornbill seals herself into a tree hole with mud and droppings, and the male feeds her and the chicks through a narrow slit; the wall keeps snakes, monkeys and other egg thieves out, and when the chicks are big the mother breaks out and the chicks seal the hole again behind her.
fact: In Kenya, hornbills and dwarf mongooses team up: the birds catch insects the mongooses disturb and call out warnings of hawks.
fact: «Rafiki» means friend in Swahili.
exercise: Are real spotted hyenas lazy scroungers that only steal food? | No, they are skilled hunters that catch most of their own food | Yes, they never hunt at all, they just wait for others to kill | Yes, they eat only the scraps that lions leave behind them | Yes, they eat only plants
exercise: What did scientists find in the Ngorongoro Crater? | Lions ate hyena kills more often than hyenas ate lion kills | Hyenas never came anywhere near the lions or their food | Hyenas ate all the lions' food | Lions and hyenas shared every single meal together as friends
exercise: What does a hyena's giggle usually mean? | It is nervous, excited or frustrated | It has heard a joke | It is very happy and full after a big meal | It is calling its cubs to bed
exercise: Who is in charge in a spotted hyena clan? | The bigger females | The biggest and strongest of all the males | The youngest cub in the clan | Nobody, there are no leaders
exercise: Why do dry hyena droppings turn white? | They are full of calcium from the bones they eat | Hyenas eat chalk | The hot sun bleaches all animal droppings white in a few days | Hyenas eat white flowers
exercise: Which animals are hyenas more closely related to? | Cats and mongooses | Dogs and wolves | Bears and pandas | Pigs and warthogs
exercise: What does the aardwolf, a small hyena, mostly eat? | Termites | Zebras | Bones | Fruit
exercise: Where do real mandrills live? | In the rainforests of Central Africa | On the open savanna | In the dry deserts of the Arabian Peninsula | In the mountains of Asia
exercise: Whose tail is Rafiki's long tail more like? | A baboon's | A mandrill's | A ring-tailed lemur's | A pet cat's
exercise: Which mandrill has the brightest face colours? | The most important male in the group | The youngest female | Every mandrill has exactly the same colours | The oldest baby
exercise: Why does a male mandrill yawn wide to show huge teeth? | To warn rivals to back off | Because it is sleepy | To cool down | To show it is hungry
exercise: Which is the biggest monkey in the world? | The mandrill | The olive baboon | The howler monkey | The proboscis monkey
exercise: Where do real red-billed hornbills feed? | On the ground of dry savannas | High up in rainforest trees only | In rivers and lakes, catching fish | On the rocky cliffs by the sea
exercise: Why is a hornbill's huge bill not too heavy? | It is hollow inside with thin bone | It is made of soft feathers | It is full of air bubbles of fat | It is made of horn like a cow's
exercise: Why does a female hornbill seal her nest hole with mud? | To keep snakes, monkeys and egg thieves out | To stop the heavy rain getting in during storms | To keep the male out | To keep the chicks quiet
exercise: How do hornbills and dwarf mongooses help each other? | Hornbills catch insects the mongooses stir up and warn of hawks | Mongooses carry the hornbills on their backs to find fresh insects | Hornbills feed mongooses fruit | They share the same nest hole
test: «hyenas are just dogs that laugh, everyone knows» What is right? | They look like dogs but are closer to cats and mongooses, and the giggle shows nerves | Right, they are a kind of wild dog | Right, they laugh at jokes | Right, they are a kind of wolf, and the giggle is how they laugh at the other animals
test: «Shenzi's the boss, but real hyena bosses are males, right» What is right? | No, in real clans the females are in charge | Right, the biggest and strongest male always rules | Right, hyenas have kings | No, the cubs are in charge
test: «2 hornbills + 3 mongooses, how many legs» How many? | 16 | 10 | 20 | 14
test: «4 hyenas fighting over 1 zebra, how many tails in that mess» How many? | 5 | 4 | 1 | 8
test: What would happen if a snake reached a hornbill's nest hole? | The mud wall leaves only a slit too narrow to get in | It would eat the chicks easily through the open hole | The mother would fly away at once | The male would push it in
test: «Rafiki lives on the savanna like a real mandrill» What is right? | Real mandrills live in rainforests; savanna monkeys like that are baboons | Right, mandrills live only on open grassy savannas, just like in the film | Right, mandrills live with lions | Wrong, mandrills live in deserts
test: «mandrill showed its teeth at me in the zoo, it hates me» What is the best answer? | A wide yawn with teeth is a warning to keep back, not hate | It was smiling at you because it likes you a lot | It was hungry for you | It was showing its teeth so that a keeper would clean them
test: «hyena just crunched a whole bone lol is it broken» What is right? | Its strong bite can crush and digest bones | Yes, its jaw must be broken | Hyenas cannot eat bones | It will be very sick afterwards, as bones are poison to hyenas
test: When the hornbill chicks are big enough, what happens to the sealed nest? | The mother breaks out, and the chicks seal it again behind her | The chicks stay inside the sealed nest forever, even when grown | The male breaks it from outside | The tree falls down
test: «hornbill waiting outside the mongoose hole in the morning, weird» Why might it wait? | It hunts insects the mongooses stir up when they wake | It wants to catch and eat the young mongooses | It is lost | It is guarding its own eggs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the feeding of the spotted hyena. | It is a proficient hunter which, in many regions, procures the greater part of its own food | It obtains its food exclusively by theft from lions, being itself incapable of hunting | It subsists entirely upon vegetation | It is incapable of the pursuit of large prey
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the social organisation of the spotted hyena? | The clan is governed by females, which exceed the males in size and strength | The clan is governed by a single male, being the largest and strongest of its members | The clan possesses no hierarchy of any kind | The clan is governed by the youngest cubs
exam: Identify the natural habitat of the mandrill. | The rainforests of Central Africa | The open grassy savannas of East Africa | The deserts of the Arabian peninsula | The high cold mountains of Central Asia
exam: Ascertain the purpose for which the female red-billed hornbill seals the entrance to her nest with mud. | The exclusion of snakes, monkeys and other thieves of eggs | The exclusion of rainwater during the wet season | The exclusion of the male from the nest | The suppression of noise made by the chicks
